"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n2le03ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Juan</strong>: Good morning, I am looking for one of the tutors, her name is Juliana.<br/>\n<strong>Juliana</strong>: That's me. I'm helping you with the tutoring session for today, which is about functional languages, right?<br/>\n<strong>Juan</strong>: Yes, it's about that topic. I don't think I'm ready for the exam yet.<br/>\n<strong>Juliana</strong>: That's ok, let's start. A functional language is an artificial language that a computer or any other programmable machine executes. In other words, a functional language is a computer language.<br/>\n<strong>Juan</strong>: OK, it seems you are using familiar words to explain the topic. The teacher always uses complex vocabulary.<br/>"}, {"time": "39000", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Juliana</strong>: That's why tutoring helps students most of the times. We are implementing one rule: the simpler, the better. Let's go back to the topic. In today's world, programmers always use functional languages, which are programming languages based on mathematical functions in all their structures. These mathematical functions possess a mathematical schema derived from lambda calculus and combinatory logic.<br/>\n<strong>Juan</strong>: Why are they always using mathematical functions?<br/>\n<strong>Juliana</strong>: Well, by using mathematical functions, functional languages become real. In other words, they are readable. This has to do with first class functions; it means that, as you are passing functions as paradigms, you receive them as values. That's why they are real.<br/>\n<strong>Juan</strong>: I understand. Please, give me an example of a functional language.<br/>"}, {"time": "96000", "image": "enpe7n2le03ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Juliana</strong>: Well, one of the most common functional languages that exist is the one programmers are using in applications and program development. Basically, it refers to programs you are working with on a regular basis. For instance, the internet browser you always use. These browsers have a functional language or computer languages such as C, C++, D, Java, and Visual Basic. Another example is the internet and web page development; programmers usually use the same functional languages such as HDML, HTML, Java, JavaScript and Python."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n2le03ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}